{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sadistic-disdainful-firefly","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\nThe \"sweep (windows)\" job of run 44211859981 (main at fc446d89df7, job 134491948817) ended `2 failed, 19627 passed`.\n1. `tests/unit/test_crash_guard.py::test_xdist_thread_timeout_names_itself_and_the_test` failed. The nested run printed `[gw0] node down: Not properly terminated` and the crash-guard line, but no `+++ Timeout +++` line.\n2. `tests/unit/test_store_commit_maintenance_bd66.py::test_write_survives_foreground_maintenance_repack` failed with `worker 'gw3' crashed` and no Timeout banner. In the previous Windows run (44141097601, at 83ca81e709e) this test passed on gw3.\n\nBoth failures come after delighted-worthless-foal's change 23e8764dada. That change makes `tests/_crash_guard.py` write the timeout banner to the worker's fd 2.\n\n## Root cause (confirmed 2026-11-17 PST)\n- execnet's worker bootstrap redirects the worker's file descriptors in `init_popen_io` (`execnet/gateway_base.py:1786-1795`):\n  - fd 1 goes to the null device on every platform.\n  - Only when `os.name == \"nt\"` does it also do `sys.stderr = fdopen(os.dup(2))` and then `os.dup2(<null fd>, 2)`.\n- So on Windows, fd 2 in a worker is NUL, and the real stderr is reachable only through the `sys.stderr` object. On POSIX, fd 2 stays the real stderr.\n- `_announce_then_expire` writes with `os.write(2, …)` and `faulthandler.dump_traceback(file=2, …)`. On Windows both go to NUL.\n- Reproduced on macOS: a nested `-n 2 -p _crash_guard -o timeout=2 -o timeout_method=thread` run, with a `sitecustomize` that applies execnet's Windows branch in the worker (`sys.argv == ['-c']`: dup fd 2 into `sys.stderr`, then point fd 2 at `os.devnull`), prints no `+++ Timeout +++` line. Without the emulation, the same run prints it.\n\n## Effect on failure 2\nThe crash guard's timer fires only after `settings.timeout`: 600 s, the CI value (`--timeout=600`). Before 23e8764dada, pytest-timeout's own timer exited the worker the same way. So the change does not make bd66 die where it would not have before. It only lost the banner that would say whether gw3 died from a timeout.\n\nWhether bd66 really ran past 600 s on Windows is not established from this log. That is checked after this fix lands, on a fresh Windows sweep, and filed separately if it did.\n\n## Approach\nIn `_announce_then_expire`:\n- After `capman.suspend_global_capture(item)`, write the banner to `sys.stderr` instead of fd 2, and flush. Suspending capture puts back the `sys.stderr` that was in place before capture: execnet's dup of the real stderr on Windows, the process stderr on POSIX.\n- Dump the thread stacks with `faulthandler.dump_traceback(file=sys.stderr, all_threads=True)`. faulthandler takes a file object and uses its `fileno()`, which is the dup on Windows and fd 2 on POSIX.\n- Everything else stays as it is: the hook, the timer, `timeout_timer`'s `os._exit(1)`.\n- No config key, env var or flag.\n\n## Scope\n- `tests/_crash_guard.py`: `_announce_then_expire` writes through `sys.stderr`, and the module docstring is corrected to say the controller-connected stream is `sys.stderr`, not fd 2.\n- `tests/unit/test_crash_guard.py`: add a nested xdist test that runs with a test-written `sitecustomize.py`. The file is generated under `tmp_path` and its directory is put first on the child's `PYTHONPATH`. It applies execnet's Windows stderr branch in workers only. The test asserts a line containing both `Timeout` and the test's node id. It fails on origin/main on POSIX hosts, so CI catches a regression without a Windows runner.\n\n## Testing\n- The new Windows-shaped test fails on origin/main's `tests/_crash_guard.py` and passes after the change.\n- The existing xdist and single-process timeout tests in `tests/unit/test_crash_guard.py` still pass.\n- After merge: `gh workflow run test.yml --ref main`, and the \"sweep (windows)\" result is recorded on this ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With execnet's Windows stderr redirection applied, an xdist thread-method timeout still prints `+++ Timeout +++ … <nodeid>` in the controller output. Checked by the new test, which fails without the fix.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0, and the change merges with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.\n- [ ] A post-merge `test.yml` run's \"sweep (windows)\" job no longer fails `test_xdist_thread_timeout_names_itself_and_the_test`. The job id and result are recorded on this ticket, along with what any bd66 failure's banner says.","id":"c691-bbc1-4217-4cf4","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Crash guard's xdist timeout banner is lost on Windows (worker fd 2 is NUL there)"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1794968224290159493,"uuid":"895fbd53-4142-44db-8b72-bd0a3f527d3e"}